radius `romanWeight(f) - 1` reaches the entire graph, leading to `eccentricity(u) <= romanWeight(f) - 1` and subsequently, `radius(G) <= romanWeight(f) - 1`.  When the distance between two vertices is greater than 2, intermediate vertices are in `V1`, which increases the Roman weight. This also means  the weight is at least  `dist(u, u2) + 1`. Analyzing the path between these vertices further indicates that a ball centered at an intermediate vertex will cover all of `V0`.\n\n\n**Refining Proof by Contradiction**\n\nThe strategy shifted to leveraging a ball coverage property and contradiction. It considers whether `ballWithin G (romanWeight f - 1) v` covers the entire graph (`univ`). If not, its cardinality is greater than or equal to `romanWeight f`.\n\nAssuming `radius(G) + 1 > romanWeight(f)`, it follows that no ball of radius `romanWeight(f) - 1` covers the whole graph. Therefore, for every vertex, the ball's cardinality is at least `romanWeight(f)`. This is examined in terms of the relationship between this ball and radius.\n\n\n**Analyzing Ball Cardinality and Graph Structure**\n\nIf the radius plus one exceeds the Roman weight, then the ball of radius `romanWeight(f) - 1` around any vertex doesn't span the entire graph, and its cardinality is at least `romanWeight(f)`.  Since the graph has no isolated vertices, every vertex's 1-ball contains at least two elements.\n\nSince the Roman weight is less than the number of vertices, and there exist vertices with value `2`, a contradiction must be derived from the remaining conditions in `prove_e20263a9`. These conditions involve relationships between graph radius, Roman weight, and specific vertices. Analyzing the ball sizes again in `test_full.lean`.\n\n\n**Identifying the Source of the `sorry`**\n\nThe `sorry` arises in a branch where `Fintype.card V > GraphCalc.romanWeight f`, `GraphCalc.radius G > 1`, and `GraphCalc.romanWeight f > 2`.  The goal now becomes proving the inequality `radius G + 1 ≤ romanWeight f` in