ls are non-empty because the radius can be greater than zero. Now investigating how many shells a single vertex of weight 2 can dominate, it appears to be at most 3 consecutive shells.\n\n\n**Analyzing Dominating Shells**\n\nA vertex with a weight of 2 can dominate at most three consecutive shells, while a weight of 1 dominates only its shell. Selecting one vertex from each shell, it's determined each vertex must be dominated.\n\nThis leads to the inequality $r + 1 \\le 3 |V_2| + |V_1|$, where r + 1 is the number of shells. Earlier, it was established $r+1 ≤ (3/2)W$. The focus shifts toward whether $r + 1 \\le W$ is obtainable, analyzing when a vertex with weight 2 dominates three vertices in the selected set. Checking for graphs where the inequality does not hold.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/test_branch.lean","ContentOffset":0,"EndLine":100,"StartLine":1,"toolAction":"Viewing test_branch.lean","toolSummary":"View test_branch.lean"}}]}
